The Excel sheet contains 10,000 data points generated using the regular solution and quasi-chemical thermodynamic model. 

Abstract:
For the chosen thermodynamic model, the phase diagram depends on the thermodynamic parameters of the
elements and the selected interaction energy parameters. A systematic variation of the energy parameters can reveal
the nature of the phase diagrams obtained under the given conditions. In the present study, the quasi-chemical
solution model, which accounts for short range order parameter up to the pair cluster, is adopted for the solid
phase, while the regular solution model is used for the liquid phase. Using these solution models, the boundaries
of the interaction energy parameters that result in various invariant reactions are calculated for the model system.
The regions bounded by these boundaries are referred to as domains. This methodology is applied to the Cu-Ir
system. The invariant reaction data obtained from these domains are used to train an artificial neural network
model to predict the interaction energy parameters. The phase diagram obtained from the predicted values shows a
good agreement with the experimental data. Thus, this methodology provides intelligent initial interaction energy
parameters for the optimization of the experimental data.

Conclusions:
Using the quasi-chemical solution approach the solid phase is modelled, while the high temperature liquid
phase is modelled using the regular solution model. The invariant reactions in the phase diagram are treated as
the interaction of the miscibility gap of one of the phases with the solidus, liquidus, or iso-G boundaries. Using
the appropriate thermodynamic conditions for the chosen solution models, the loci of energies corresponding to
congruent minimum, congruent maximum, eutectic, peritectic, monotectic, and syntectic reactions are determined
for a prototype binary system. The isothermal contours of these invariant reactions, illustrating the behaviour of
the system within their respective domains are also presented.
The developed methodology is applied to the Cu-Ir binary system, which exhibits a peritectic reaction with a
miscibility gap in the solid phase at low temperatures. The existence domain boundaries are calculated for this
system, and the invariant reaction data corresponding to the peritectic domain are generated. These data are used
to train a supervised artificial neural network model to predict the energy parameters of both solid and liquid phases.
In the model, temperature (T) and compositions (xα
Ir, xβ
Ir) served as inputs, while energy parameters (wα, wβ) are
predicted as outputs. The neural network optimization reveals that a network architecture with two hidden layers
is sufficient to capture the complex correlations between inputs and outputs. The phase diagram calculated using
the neural network predicted energy parameters shows a good agreement with the experimentally reported phase
diagram for the Cu-Ir system, with some deviations in solubility on the Ir-rich side. These deviations are attributed
to the limited number of energy parameters in the selected solution model. Thus, the proposed methodology can
be used to get a reliable set of initial values for the optimization of experimental or theoretical phase diagram
data as well as thermodynamic data. 
Extension of the methodology to other alloy systems would require
retraining with appropriate data such as elemental and thermodynamic descriptors to improve model transferability
and enable the development of more generalized predictive frameworks.
It should be noted that the linear least-squares model employed for comparison serves as a simple
reference baseline intended to highlight the benefits of nonlinear learning.
Future work may
consider comparisons with more advanced inversion and regression methodologies to further assess the relative
strengths and limitations of neural-network-based approaches. It also includes extending the developed methodology
for existence domain calculations for more complex solution models that provide short range order parameters
and are computationally intensive. Furthermore, the supervised neural network model for predicting the energy
parameters can be integrated with multiscale computational frameworks, including density functional theory (DFT),
CALPHAD, and phase-field modeling for microstructure evolution and property prediction (Kumar Thakur et al.,
2023; Morino et al., 2025; Shen, 2025). Advanced architectures such as graph neural networks and long short
term memory (LSTM) networks can also be explored to enhance predictive accuracy and enable inverse design of
alloy compositions (Qin et al., 2023; Razavi and Moelans, 2026). These extensions are expected to broaden the
applicability of the approach presented in this work to complex materials systems and accelerate data-driven alloy
development.


Comments:
The same 20% subset was used for: architecture selection and model evaluation. The authors now openly admit this. That honesty is appreciated, but scientifically the issue remains. A proper workflow would require train, validation, independent test or cross-validation.

Reviewer #2 requested comparison against direct inversion. The authors only compare against linear least-squares fitting. This is still a relatively weak benchmark. The paper therefore demonstrates: NN is better than linear regression but not necessarily NN is better than existing thermodynamic inversion methods. This concern is partially addressed but not completely resolved.

The NN is learning a smooth inverse surrogate of the forward model. That is acceptable for the paper’s purpose, but the novelty should not be overstated.




Final address of the comments:



Response to comment 1:
We thank the reviewer for this important observation and fully agree that a strict machine learning workflow ideally requires separate training, validation, and independent testing datasets, or alternatively a cross-validation-based model selection strategy.
In the present study, the available dataset corresponding to the invariant reaction existence domain of the Cu-Ir system was relatively limited. To maximize the amount of data available for model training, the 20% subset was used both for architecture selection and for final performance assessment. We acknowledge that this approach may introduce a degree of optimistic bias in the reported predictive performance.
To address this concern, we have explicitly stated this limitation in the revised manuscript and clarified that the neural network model is intended primarily as a proof-of-concept framework for demonstrating the feasibility of learning the inverse mapping between invariant reaction characteristics and interaction energy parameters. 
We appreciate the reviewer's comment, which has helped us clarify the scope and limitations of the current study.
We can add the following paragraph either in discussion / conclusions / future work section:
"It should be noted that the neural network architecture was selected using the same 20% hold-out test dataset employed for model evaluation because of the limited size of the available dataset. While this strategy was adopted to maximize the training data, it may lead to optimistic estimates of predictive performance. Future studies will employ (A more robust study employs) separate validation and independent test datasets or cross-validation-based model selection procedures to provide a more rigorous assessment of model generalization."
Response to comment 2:
We agree that comparison against a full thermodynamic inversion framework would provide a stronger benchmark. However, the objective of the present work is not to replace established thermodynamic parameter optimization approaches. Rather, the neural network is intended to serve as a computationally efficient inverse surrogate capable of generating physically meaningful initial estimates of interaction parameters. Conventional thermodynamic inversion methods remain the standard route for final parameter optimization and database assessment. A detailed comparison with such methods is beyond the scope of the present proof-of-concept study and will be the subject of future work.
In response to the reviewer's concern, we have revised the manuscript to moderate the claims regarding novelty and performance. We state that the proposed neural network should be viewed as a complementary tool capable of generating informed initial estimates of interaction energy parameters, which can subsequently be refined using conventional thermodynamic optimization procedures.
We have additionally expanded the discussion to acknowledge that future studies should compare the proposed framework with more advanced inverse-design and thermodynamic parameter optimization methods to establish its relative advantages and limitations more comprehensively.
We appreciate the reviewer's suggestion and believe that the revised text now better reflects the intended scope of the work.
We can add the following in the discussion / conclusion section:
"The proposed neural-network model should be viewed as an inverse surrogate framework for generating physically reasonable initial estimates of interaction energy parameters rather than as a replacement for established thermodynamic inversion and optimization methodologies. The comparison presented in this work against linear least-squares regression serves primarily as a baseline illustrating the advantages of nonlinear learning. Future investigations should include comparisons with more sophisticated inversion and thermodynamic optimization approaches to further assess the strengths and limitations of neural-network-based parameter prediction."


